<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congo Arab war</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congo Arab war was fought from 1892 to 1894 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> and the Swahili-Arab traders of the eastern Congo, whose power had been built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tippu-tip">Tippu Tip</a>. Free State columns under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-dhanis">Francis Dhanis</a>, aided by African allies, took the trading towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyangwe">Nyangwe</a> and Kasongo in 1893 and defeated the leaders Sefu and Rumaliza, bringing the east under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>'s control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congo-arab-war.jpg" alt="Congo Arab war" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BorysMapping · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arab_Congo_War.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>At first the Free State worked with the Arab-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> traders. In 1887, at the proposal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tippu-tip">Tippu Tip</a> became governor of the Stanley Falls District under Free State authority, but he behaved more as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> than an official, and rival traders such as Rumaliza of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a> called the deal treason. The two sides competed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> had pledged at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; the war came to be presented as a Christian anti-slavery <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>. When Tippu Tip left for Zanzibar in 1890, his son Sefu bin Hamid ruled from Kasongo and his nephew Rashid bin Mohammad held Stanley Falls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In March and April 1892 Sefu's men attacked Free State personnel, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> trader Arthur Hodister, who was killed. Ngongo Lutete, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetela-people">Batetela</a> and Bakusu, went over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/force-publique">Force Publique</a> on 19 September 1892, and other local leaders followed. Open war began in late November. Rumaliza's troops besieged the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> post of Albertville on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a> for nine months until a relief column arrived.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyangwe">Nyangwe</a> fell in March 1893, a rising there ended with most of the town set ablaze; an officer, Sidney Langford Hinde, wrote that a town of about thirty thousand inhabitants had been reduced to one fortified house. Kasongo fell in two hours on 22 April. Commandant Pierre Ponthier was killed attacking Rumaliza's forts. Sefu died in the battle on the Luama River on 20 October 1893, and on 9 January 1894 a shell blew up Rumaliza's ammunition store; more than two thousand prisoners were taken, but Rumaliza escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>. The last Zanzibari force was captured in December 1894 and all its members were executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war caused tens of thousands of deaths among combatants and civilians. The market around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyangwe">Nyangwe</a> ceased to exist and Kasongo was all but destroyed, so much of the Congo's trade was turned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malebo-pool">Stanley Pool</a> and the Atlantic. The colonial authorities kept much of the Arab administrative structure in the east until the 1920s, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetela-people">Batetela</a> and Bakusu, mistrusted for their part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, were neglected under colonial rule. The historian Bruce Vandervort called it one of the most obscure wars of imperial conquest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congo Arab war” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Congo Arab war</b> of 1892–1894 destroyed the power of the Swahili-Arab traders of the eastern Congo, the network of Tippu Tip, whom the state had appointed governor of the Stanley Falls district in 1887; columns under Francis Dhanis took Nyangwe and Kasongo in 1893.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
